---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import WebSourceDemo from '../../components/WebSourceDemo';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Docs title="Web Source" description="A chip for a web page: its favicon and title, linking to the page.">
  <h1 class="docs-title">Web Source</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    Shows a web page an agent searched or read: the site's favicon and the page title, linking to the page in a new tab.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8">
    <WebSourceDemo client:load />
  </div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Usage</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Web Source usage"
      code={`import { WebSource } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/web-source";

<WebSource url="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Accessibility" title="Accessibility | MDN" />

// No title: shows the address without https://www.
<WebSource url="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/" />

// The page could not be read
<WebSource url="https://www.ft.com/content/inflation" failed />

// Your own icon service, or () => null for a globe
<WebSource url={url} icon={(page) => \`/api/favicon?url=\${encodeURIComponent(page)}\`} />`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>url</code> is the page's address. Only <code>http</code> and <code>https</code> addresses become links,
      which open in a new tab; other addresses show a plain chip with a globe. <code>title</code> is the page title,
      usually from a search result or a page read. A browser cannot read another site's title itself. Without one, the
      chip shows the address without <code>https://www.</code>. Long titles are cut off with an ellipsis; the full title
      and address show on hover.
    </p>
    <p>
      The favicon comes from <code>webSourceIcon</code>, which asks icon.horse for the page's site. Pass
      <code>icon</code> to use another service. It returns an image address for a page, or nothing for a globe. A globe
      shows while the icon loads and stays if it fails.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>failed</code> adds a warning icon for a page that could not be read.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Icons load without cookies or a referrer, so the icon service learns the site's name but not who asked or from
      which page. The service must allow cross-origin requests; icon.horse does.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      The chip is a link named by its title. The icon is decorative. A failed page also says “Page unavailable. Retry or
      paste the text.” to screen readers. On touch screens the chip is at least 44px tall.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Theme tokens</h2>
    <p>
      The chip uses <code>--sprout-secondary</code>, <code>--sprout-hover</code> and <code>--sprout-active</code>. The
      icon tile uses <code>--sprout-surface</code>; the warning uses <code>--sprout-danger</code>. Radius follows
      <code>--sprout-radius-control</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
